{"paper_id": "paper-ca74ee97a9759f9f", "doi": "10.1021/acs.jmedchem.6c00260", "title": "Configurationally Locked Ridaifen-B Analogs as Potent Ebola Virus Entry Inhibitors.", "analysis": {"analysis": {"research_question_and_background": "Ebola virus disease remains a global public health threat due to emerging outbreaks, high mortality rates, and limited treatment options, necessitating the discovery of effective antiviral therapies.", "study_design_and_population": "The study designed, synthesized, and evaluated a series of cyclized ridaifen-B-based inhibitors targeting Ebola virus glycoprotein (GP) to assess their antiviral activity and selectivity.", "methods": "The authors synthesized cyclized ridaifen-B analogs and evaluated their potency against Ebola virus GP-mediated entry using antiviral assays, followed by site-directed mutagenesis to identify the binding site at the Ebola virus GP1/GP2 fusion loop.", "main_results": "Several cyclized ridaifen-B analogs exhibited nanomolar potency inhibiting Ebola virus GP-mediated entry and submicromolar potency against Marburg virus entry, with inhibitor binding localized to the Ebola virus GP1/GP2 fusion loop.", "interpretation_and_novelty": "The study identifies 1,2-dihydronaphthalene and tetrahydronaphthalene derivatives as promising leads for antifiloviral drug development, highlighting their potential as Ebola virus entry inhibitors with favorable pharmacokinetic properties.", "scientific_and_public_health_significance": "These findings support the development of ridaifen-B-based inhibitors as candidate therapies for Ebola virus disease, addressing the urgent need for effective antiviral treatments.", "limitations_and_evidence_strength": "The evidence is limited to in vitro and early pharmacokinetic assessments, with no reported in vivo efficacy or safety data, warranting cautious interpretation of the therapeutic potential."}, "summary_en": "This study investigates cyclized ridaifen-B analogs as potential antiviral agents targeting Ebola virus entry by inhibiting the viral glycoprotein (GP)-mediated fusion process. The authors synthesized and evaluated a series of compounds, demonstrating nanomolar potency against Ebola virus entry and submicromolar activity against Marburg virus. Site-directed mutagenesis localized inhibitor binding to the Ebola virus GP1/GP2 fusion loop, supporting a mechanism of action. While the compounds showed favorable in vitro pharmacokinetic parameters, the evidence is confined to laboratory studies without in vivo validation, leaving therapeutic efficacy and safety unproven. The results highlight 1,2-dihydronaphthalene and tetrahydronaphthalene derivatives as promising leads for antifiloviral drug development.", "evidence_ids": {"research_question_and_background": ["A1"], "study_design_and_population": ["A3"], "methods": ["A3", "A5"], "main_results": ["A4", "A5"], "interpretation_and_novelty": ["A7"], "scientific_and_public_health_significance": ["A1"], "limitations_and_evidence_strength": ["A6"]}, "confidence": "moderate", "postprocess_audit": {"removed_duplicate_sentences": 0, "repaired_incomplete_fields": [], "evidence_replacements": [], "policy": "v10-exclusive-rhetorical-fields-1"}, "language_contract": {"policy_version": "v17.1-source-language-safe-english-1", "source_language": "en", "verified_english": true, "replacements": [], "original_evidence_preserved": false}, "status": "passed", "kind": "research", "provider": "mistral", "model": "mistral-small-latest", "attempts": [{"task": "paper_research_analysis", "provider": "gemini", "model": "", "at": "2026-08-16T21:28:35+00:00", "status": "skipped", "failure_category": "authentication_failed", "error": "authentication_failed"}, {"task": "paper_research_analysis", "provider": "bigmodel", "model": "glm-4.5", "at": "2026-08-16T21:28:35+00:00", "status": "skipped", "failure_category": "model_cooldown", "error": "model is temporarily cooling down"}, {"task": "paper_research_analysis", "provider": "b